From 0abf8bde12114e35c8b34bef4c05ea0a0848c75f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nick Alonso <nick@drkm.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 22:48:47 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 04/10] Inkling: bounded window caches for the sliding-window
 layers

make_cache gave every layer an unbounded KVCache although the 35 sliding
layers of Inkling-Small slice their keys to the last sliding_window_size - 1
positions before the mask and attention (the distance bias and the window
cap never reach further). That cost 4 KB per token per layer for keys no
layer could read: 4.7 GB at 32K context and 18.8 GB at 128K on top of the
weights, and a 256-step reallocation of the whole buffer. Sliding layers
now get a SlidingWindowKVCache sized to the window; global layers keep the
full KVCache. The attention code is unchanged: the window cache returns the
newest keys in temporal order, so the existing slice to L + window - 1 keys
and the mask offset S - L produce the same mask and the same scores. The
speculative snapshot used by speculative_verify_hidden/rollback now carries
meta_state next to state so a windowed (or quantized) cache restores its
absolute position; the batch path converts through to_batch into
BatchSlidingWindowKVCache (left padding handled as before).

Tests compare the bounded caches against the previous layout on a
600-token chunked prefill followed by hundreds of greedy decode steps (for
8- and 512-token windows, with several compactions), check the additive mask
on the bounded cache equals both the all-keys mask and the closed form
r . proj[:, distance], check speculative verify/rollback matches a replay,
and check a left-padded batch matches its rows run alone.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
